Claim 11 - Recovery loops (admit it's stuck, write what went wrong,
                           revert to a prior checkpoint, hand off
                           context to another agent)
=====================================================================

Method: Static inspection only. No CLI / live tests executed.
Internal jargon "ralph" is used freely below for traceability. It
MUST NOT appear in any README wording.

Findings:

1. The worker can self-report being stuck. The CLI worker prompt
   tells the model exactly how to do this:
   - src/goal/cli-worker.ts:170 (worker prompt template includes the
     `"status":"ralph"` response shape with approachTried,
     specificErrors, keyInsight, suggestedApproach)
   - src/goal/cli-worker.ts:659-662 (worker instructions block
     "Ralph (stuck after genuine attempt ...)" guides the agent to
     use this only when continuing is slower than reverting)
   - src/goal/cli-worker.ts:1034 (`if (output.status === "ralph")
     return "ralph";`)

2. The data shape for "what went wrong" is a typed contract, not
   freeform prose:
   - src/goal/types.ts:50-54 (`export type RalphDetail = {
     approachTried: string; specificErrors: string; keyInsight:
     string; suggestedApproach: string; };`)
   - src/goal/task-runner.ts:22-26 (TaskRunnerResult.status includes
     "ralph"; result carries ralphDetail)
   - src/goal/cli-runner.ts:100-103 (cli-runner translates worker
     output into a TaskRunnerResult with status "ralph" and the
     parsed RalphDetail)

3. The orchestrator counts ralph attempts per step and decides
   whether to retry or escalate:
   - src/goal/agent-executor.ts:82 (`const DEFAULT_MAX_RALPH_ATTEMPTS
     = 2`)
   - src/goal/agent-executor.ts:183 (`session.stepRalphCounts ??=
     {};`)
   - src/goal/agent-executor.ts:192 (`const maxRalphAttempts =
     retryConfig?.maxRalphAttempts ?? DEFAULT_MAX_RALPH_ATTEMPTS;`)
   - src/goal/agent-executor.ts:477-530 (full ralph branch:
     increments count, builds history, escalates to blocked when the
     limit is hit, otherwise resets to the task base SHA and retries
     with appended ralph context)

4. Revert to a prior checkpoint is real and uses git, not just
   in-memory state:
   - src/goal/git-checkpoint.ts:7-8 (GitResult / GitCommitResult)
   - src/goal/git-checkpoint.ts:296,311 (startTaskCheckpoint /
     finalizeTaskCheckpoint exports)
   - src/goal/agent-executor.ts:499 (`resetToTaskBaseSha(workingDir,
     session.taskCheckpoints?.[task.id]?.baseSha)` on each ralph)
   - src/goal/agent-executor.ts:386-400 (startTaskCheckpoint at task
     entry captures the base SHA the revert restores to)

5. The "what went wrong" record is persisted to disk for the run,
   not just held in memory:
   - src/goal/run-journal.ts:46-78 (appendRalphContext appends a
     numbered `## Ralph (attempt N) - <timestamp>` block to the run
     journal on each ralph)
   - src/goal/run-journal.ts:80-90 (buildRalphHistorySummary
     consolidates all attempt bundles into the blocked-question
     handoff text)
   - src/goal/agent-executor.ts:484-497 (the blocked question after
     hitting the ralph limit IS the consolidated ralph history)

6. Recovery insights survive the run as scoped lessons that future
   workers can read:
   - src/goal/lessons.ts:35,39 (LessonSource includes "ralph";
     LESSON_SOURCES set includes it)
   - src/goal/lessons.ts:415-453 (collectRalphInsights gathers
     RalphDetail from attempt bundles)
   - src/goal/lessons.ts:487-491 (later worker prompts include a
     "Ralph key insights:" preamble built from prior runs)

7. Hand-off to a DIFFERENT agent (the literal claim wording):
   - Retry-with-new-strategy: implemented. The next attempt receives
     the ralph history prepended via appendRalphContext, so the same
     worker rereads its own failure and is steered to a new plan.
   - Hand-off to a different backend / different agent: NOT
     implemented. The retry uses the same backend that was clamped
     in src/goal/agent-executor.ts:355-358 by
     clampBackendForEnabledWorkers; nothing in the ralph branch
     selects a fresh backend or spawns a second model.
   - Escalation to the operator IS the only "hand-off to another
     agent": once stepRalphCounts >= maxRalphAttempts the step
     becomes blocked with the full ralph history as
     blockedQuestion, and the operator becomes the next agent in
     the loop.

Implementation summary:
- The system DOES admit it is stuck (typed RalphDetail emitted from
  the worker).
- The system DOES write what went wrong (RalphDetail + journal +
  attempt bundles + run-scoped lessons).
- The system DOES revert to a prior checkpoint (git reset to the
  task base SHA).
- The system DOES retry with a new strategy (appended ralph history
  steers the next attempt).
- The system DOES escalate to the operator when the limit is hit
  (blocked with full history).
- The system does NOT automatically hand the context off to a
  different model/agent backend; only the operator is the "next
  agent" in that scenario.

Safe README wording:
- "When a worker reports it is stuck, SmithersBot reverts the local
  checkpoint, records what went wrong, and retries the task with
  that context. After a configurable retry budget the step is
  escalated to the operator with the full failure history."

Unsafe wording to avoid:
- The internal token "ralph" must not appear in any public README.
- Do not claim "hands the work off to a second model" - the next
  agent is the operator, not another backend.
- Do not claim a recovery guarantee or unlimited retries.

STATUS: partial
REASON: The recovery loop, checkpoint revert, persisted failure
record, ralph-aware retry, escalation to operator, and lessons
extraction are all implemented and traceable. The literal "hand off
context to another agent" portion is implemented only as escalation
to the operator; there is no automatic switch to a different model
backend on stuck-loops, so the claim is partial - safe README wording
must describe operator escalation, not multi-agent handoff.
